{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9629c611b8df51f","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n22:The dashboard-operator reconciles Dashboard custom resources created by the rhods-operator, deploying and configuring the console pod alongside its BFF sidecars. The operator implements a declarative ownership model where the parent Dashboard CR controls the lifecycle of Deployments, Services, Routes, NetworkPolicies, PodDisruptionBudgets, and RBAC resources via controller-runtime's `Owns()` watches. Controller watches on platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) enable the dashboard to reflect the real-time state of the AI platform without polling. The operator uses `odh-platform-utilities` for platform detection, manifest rendering, and deployment helpers. [source: dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6, dashboard-operator/config/rbac/role.yaml:1]\n24:Authentication follows a two-layer model: kube-rbac-proxy fronts the Node.js backend (port 8443) performing OpenShift OAuth validation and injecting identity headers (`x-forwarded-access-token`, `x-auth-request-user`, `x-auth-request-groups`), while each Go BFF sidecar independently validates Bearer tokens and performs SubjectAccessReviews against the Kubernetes API for fine-grained authorization. The agent-ops BFF extends this with per-agent RBAC via SubjectAccessReviews for agent-specific access control. The Node.js backend additionally proxies Kubernetes API calls (`/api/k8s/*`) by converting user tokens to impersonation headers, allowing the frontend to interact with the cluster API through the dashboard service boundary. [source: backend/src/utils/constants.ts:18-20, backend/src/utils/proxy.ts:39-42, packages/agent-ops/bff/internal/api/middleware.go:1]\n269:| Kubernetes API (nodes) | list | unknown | nodes resource access via RBAC |\n307:### RBAC - Cluster Roles\n362:| odh-dashboard-operator-role | feast.dev | featurestores | get, list, watch |\n418:| dashboard-operator-role | feast.dev | featurestores | get, list, watch |\n424:### RBAC - Role Bindings\n428:| odh-dashboard-operator-rolebinding | system | odh-dashboard-operator-role (ClusterRole) | odh-dashboard-operator |\n429:| dashboard-operator-rolebinding | system | dashboard-operator-role (ClusterRole) | dashboard-operator |\n446:| Kubernetes API | REST | ServiceAccount token (in-cluster) | kube-apiserver | RBAC enforced via odh-dashboard-operator-role ClusterRole; SA odh-dashboard-operator |\n453:| /gen-ai/api/v1/* | ALL | Bearer Token (x-forwarded-access-token) | Go BFF middleware (RequireAccessToService) | RBAC and namespace access |\n455:| /agent-ops/api/v1/agents/* | GET | Bearer Token + SubjectAccessReview | Go BFF middleware (RequireAccessToAgent) | Per-agent RBAC via SSAR |\n456:| /api/k8s/* | ALL | Bearer Token to K8s Impersonation | Node.js proxy to K8s API | User Kubernetes RBAC |\n468:| rbac-ref | k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/authorization/authorizer | RBAC/authorization API import | dependency-signal |\n469:| rbac-ref | k8s.io/client-go/kubernetes/typed/authorization/v1 | RBAC/authorization API import | dependency-signal |\n505:- **User request path:** Browser clients connect to the dashboard through an OpenShift Route that terminates TLS and forwards traffic to kube-rbac-proxy (port 8443). Kube-rbac-proxy validates the user's OpenShift OAuth token and injects identity headers (`x-forwarded-access-token`, `x-auth-request-user`, `x-auth-request-groups`) before forwarding to the Node.js Fastify backend. The backend serves the React SPA for UI requests at `/` and proxies `/api/k8s/*` requests to the Kubernetes API using user token impersonation. Module federation remotes are served at `/_mf/:name/*` paths, loading domain-specific UI bundles dynamically. [source: backend/src/utils/constants.ts:18-20, backend/src/utils/proxy.ts:39-42, backend/src/routes/root.ts:1, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n507:- **Controller reconciliation:** The dashboard-operator watches the Dashboard CR (created by rhods-operator) and reconciles the deployment of the console pod, RBAC resources, routes, and services. It also watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to propagate platform state changes into dashboard configuration. The Workspace controller reconciles Workspace and WorkspaceKind CRs, managing StatefulSets, Services, and PodDisruptionBudgets for notebook workspaces. [source: dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, dashboard-operator/config/rbac/role.yaml:1]\n545:| Kubernetes API | API client | runtime-integration | 6443 | HTTPS | TLS 1.2+ | Cluster resource management via RBAC |\n547:| Kubernetes API (nodes) | list |  |  |  | Unknown | nodes resource access via RBAC |\n602:| rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1/ClusterRole | Resource read |  |  |  | Unknown | list operations by DashboardReconciler |\n603:| rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1/ClusterRoleBinding | Resource read |  |  |  | Unknown | list operations by DashboardReconciler |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bbdd7d948b51495","agent_id":"adbe888c35f5fced7","session_id":"6ff6a9df-bf42-414a-b8db-47534bab81b1","uuid":"8858125d-ccbb-4537-8e0e-2e840d46b96e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:01:31.225Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9629c611b8df51f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8007"}
