{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-80f91d18-c96c-4469-9d87-211eddb6e467","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d0b08b7801888dc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8007-yva43hx5/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8007-E003.md","content":"# Dashboard RBAC extension and feature-flag manifests for Feature Store admin operations\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the odh-dashboard's RBAC and configuration manifests so platform administrators can create, update, patch, and delete `FeatureStore` custom resources (feast.dev/v1) through the dashboard, and gate the new admin UI behind an `OdhDashboardConfig` feature flag for progressive rollout. This covers the backend/manifests portion of the strategy's odh-dashboard changes — no new backend routes are needed, since CRUD uses the existing `/api/k8s/*` pass-through proxy that forwards the authenticated user's token to the Kubernetes API.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Update `manifests/core-bases/base/sa-rbac/cluster-role.yaml` to add `create, update, patch, delete` verbs for `feast.dev/featurestores` to the dashboard's ClusterRole (the existing role already grants `get, list, watch`). The `featurestore-editor-role` ClusterRole defined by the feast-operator (architecture context: `feast.md`, RBAC section) serves as the reference for the minimum required permission set.\n- Validate the RBAC mechanism assumption flagged \"needs validation\" in the strategy: confirm whether extending the dashboard ClusterRole is the correct mechanism for granting platform admins write access, given that CRUD requests through `/api/k8s/*` are authorized against the user's own forwarded token (architecture context: `odh-dashboard.md`, Authentication & Authorization), or whether admin role bindings (e.g., binding the feast-operator's editor role) are required instead. Implement the validated mechanism.\n- Add the feature flag default in `OdhDashboardConfig` (e.g., a `featureStore` flag) gating the admin UI; default off so clusters without the Feast component enabled see no UI changes.\n- Ensure the viewer tier continues to work read-only through the existing `featurestore-viewer-role` ClusterRole from the feast-operator (get, list, watch on featurestores and featurestores/status) — no changes to viewer permissions.\n- Manifest-only changes: no new Go BFF module, preserving FIPS compliance (Node.js 22 / UBI 9 base) per the strategy's NFRs.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A platform administrator can create and delete `FeatureStore` CRs from the dashboard via the `/api/k8s/*` pass-through using their own token, measured by successful CRUD operations performed as an admin through the dashboard proxy.\n- A non-admin (viewer) token attempting to create or delete a `FeatureStore` CR is rejected by the Kubernetes API (viewer retains only get/list/watch via the existing viewer role).\n- The RBAC mechanism validation is documented (admin role bindings vs ClusterRole extension), and the implemented mechanism matches it.\n- The admin UI is gated behind an `OdhDashboardConfig` feature flag, off by default; clusters without the Feast component enabled show no UI changes.\n- No new backend routes, no new BFF sidecar container, and no new external network connections are introduced.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables the P0 HLRs \"deploy a new FeatureStore instance from the dashboard\" and \"delete a FeatureStore instance from the dashboard\" (write-path RBAC).\n- Supports the P1 viewer HLR: non-admin users keep read-only access enforced at the Kubernetes API level, not just in the UI.\n- Implements the security NFRs (K8s RBAC via forwarded bearer token, kube-rbac-proxy, `secureAdminRoute` middleware complement).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":48941,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bbdd7d948b51495",