ource consumption in QE environments) is not carried into any epic scope\n36\t    or acceptance criterion.\n37\terror: null\n38\t---\n39\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8007\n40\t\n41\t## Review Summary\n42\t\n43\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n44\tRecommendation: revise\n45\t\n46\tAuto-fail triggered: Criterion 4 (Type Correctness) scores 0 due to a major gating-metadata defect. Although the total score exceeds the pass threshold, any criterion at 0 forces `pass: false`.\n47\t\n48\t## Criterion Details\n49\t\n50\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n51\t\n52\tAll eight HLRs are mapped in the traceability matrix and verified against epic HLR Traceability sections: the five P0 HLRs map to E005 (wizard, enabled by E001/E002/E003) and E004 (list, inspect, delete, status tracking); the P1 viewer-gating and air-gapped HLRs map to E003/E004/E005; the P2 inline-validation HLR maps to E005. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 and E002 (both P0) block P0 work; E006 is a `docs-authoring` epic whose P1 derives from the strategy's Jira priority (Major → P1) per the docs-authoring exception — its dependencies on P0 implementations are not a violation. No priority collapse: the P1 viewer gating and air-gapped HLRs share UI surfaces and implementation with their P0 counterparts (not independently deferrable), and the P2 validation HLR is UX polish of the same wizard form — the \"distinct and deferrable feature\" test is correctly applied and documented. Triage is null (full decomposition), so multi-epic output is expected.\n53\t\n54\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n55\t\n56\tAll 8 diagram edges match epic frontmatter `dependencies` exactly (E001→{E002,E005}; E002→{E004,E005,E006}; E003→E006; E004→E006; E005→E006), and vice versa — no edge in one place missing from the other, no references to nonexistent epics, arrows drawn dependency→dependent per convention. No cycles. Critical path length 4 (E001→E002→{E004|E005}→E006) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4). Parallelism is handled correctly: E003 (RBAC/flag manifests) is deliberately not wired as a dependency of E004/E005 — RBAC/flag availability is enforced as acceptance-criteria gates (Rule 13/15 pattern), and the justification explicitly rejects the configuration-reference-as-edge trap. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 pairings are not applicable. No conditional branch files exist, and none are warranted (both investigation outcomes land in the same wizard epic per the summary's justification). E005's `gated_by` target (E001) appears in its direct `dependencies` list. Cross-checking epic content found no uncaptured data/artifact dependencies: E005 consumes E001's form-field spec (edge present), E004/E005 consume E002's designs (edges present), E006's screenshots require the built UI (edges present).\n57\t\n58\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n59\t\n60\tEach epic has a clean component/team tuple, all using canonical component names (AI Core Dashboard, Feature Store, UXD, Documentation — verified against `.context/rhai-components.txt`). The three odh-dashboard strategy rows are correctly decomposed by sub-system (manifests E003 / management views E004 / wizard E005) under one team rather than artificially split across teams; E001 correctly separates Feature Store Engineering's validation consultation because a different team performs it. No epic mixes code implementation with content authoring (design is E002 alone, docs are E006 alone). No epic is clearly oversized — E004 bundles list/detail/delete/status, but these are one management surface with row actions, built against an existing validated prototype; single-sprint scale is plausible. Minor observation: the Create-button visibility behavior is specified as an acceptance criterion in both E004 (\"list and detail views render without the Create button\") and E005 (\"non-admin users do not see the Create action\") — incidental overlap on one control, not duplicate construction of a surface.\n61\t\n62\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n63\t\n64\t**Major issue — missing gate metadata on E002.** E002 (UXD wireframes) lists the Investigation epic E001 in its `dependencies`, but its frontmatter has `gated_by: null` and `gate_failure_impact: null`. The decomposition's own DAG justification labels the E001→E002 edge a \"True gate (`rewrite`)\" — the investigation's field-set outcome changes what the wizard wireframes must cover — so by the criteria this is an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`, a major issue: automated pipeline consumers detect gates via `gated_by` and will silently miss this one. The inconsistency is internal to the decomposition: E005, gated by the same investigation for the same reason, correctly carries `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8007-E001` plus a populated `gate_failure_impact` (action: rewrite, with fallback approach). Fix: set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8007-E001` and a `gate_failure_impact` (action: rewrite; fallback: re-scope the wizard wireframes to the validated field set) on E002. All other typing is correct: E001 is a genuine Investigation (its field-set/backend-restriction/prototype-alignment outcomes change downstream scope, with two dependents); E002–E006 produce concrete artifacts; E005's `gated_by` is in its direct dependencies; E006's Rule 11 dependencies are scheduling edges correctly lacking gates.\n65\t\n66\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n67\t\n68\tAll six ai-signals files are present, each epic uses the rubric matching its type (E001 carries `investigation_signals`; E002–E006 carry `ai_signals`), and frontmatter values match the rationale tables. Two arguable signal values: (1) E002 scores `open_questions: 0` with the rationale that the gating unknown is \"resolved by the upstream investigation dependency,\" while E005 scores `open_questions: -1` for the same still-unresolved E001 outcome (\"gated... rather than resolved\") — E002's wizard wireframes depend on the field-set outcome just as E005's wizard does, so the inconsistent treatment is arguable; (2) E003 scores `open_questions: 0` although the RBAC mechanism (extend dashboard ClusterRole vs. admin role bindings) is flagged \"needs validation\" in the strategy and the answer changes which concrete manifest changes are implemented — the \"deliverable is the same either way\" rationale is defensible but borderline. Rationales are otherwise justified and consistent with strategy content (e.g., E005's `change_specificity: 0` correctly reflecting the unfixed field set; E006's `human_process_gates: -1` for the docs technical review).\n69\t\n70\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n71\t\n72\tEvery epic has testable, strategy-derived acceptance criteria. Rule-mandated ACs are present: E006 (docs-authoring) requires technical review by engineering before publication; feature-flag gating ACs appear in E003 (flag off by default, no UI change without Feast), E004, and E005 — exceeding the requirement since this is a new capability, not a replacement. E003's admin/viewer RBAC ACs (admin CRUD succeeds via forwarded token; viewer write rejected at the K8s API) are the correct enforcement-point checks. E004/E005 ACs mirror the strategy's given/when/then criteria including measurable checks (`kubectl get featurestores -A` match, NotFound after delete, air-gapped deployment). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by scan) — cross-references use full descriptions or the strategy's named artifacts.\n73\t\n74\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n75\t\n76\tThe combined epic set accounts for all strategy scope: create wizard (E005), list/detail/delete/status views (E004), RBAC extension and feature flag (E003), UXD design (E002), investigation of open questions (E001), and admin-guide documentation (E006 — satisfying Rule 11, as the strategy's Prerequisites list Documentation Support as needed). All seven strategy acceptance criteria have coverage; every open question has an explicit disposition (including the edit-capability question correctly resolved as out-of-scope by the strategy itself). Strategy-level context is carried forward: the UXD-delay risk mitigation (prototype PR #7013 as design reference) is in E002 scope, the CRD-stability risk is E001 scope, the viewer-RBAC-bypass risk is an E003 AC, and the air-gapped validation-completeness risk is covered by E001 (validation rules from CRD schema) and E005 (server-side error surfacing). Minor gap: the pod-resource-growth risk's mitigation (\"monitor dashboard pod resource consumption in QE environments\") is not carried into any epic's scope or ACs — a dropped secondary mitigation, not a capability gap.\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96c9028417b4aba7","agent_id":"aa3326b8e093fa5f7","session_id":"6ff6a9df-bf42-414a-b8db-47534bab81b1","uuid":"d9aa8e8e-baae-4166-b1b6-a944fd8fc50b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:10:38.760Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c495b8369abf9a5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8007 decomposition"}
