fails the \"distinct and deferrable feature\" test, so bundling is correct.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nCross-checked every edge in the mermaid diagram against epic frontmatter `dependencies` in both directions — perfect match: E001→E002, E001→E005, E002→E004, E002→E005, E002→E006, E003→E006, E004→E006, E005→E006. Arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention. No cycles, no references to nonexistent epics, no branch files (none used — both bounded investigation outcomes land in the same epics with different scope, which the summary justifies).\n\nEdge justification is sound: E001→E002/E005 are true gates (field-set outcome changes downstream scope, recorded as `gated_by`); E002→E004/E005 are mandated by the strategy's dependency table (\"form wizard and list page designs required before implementation\"); E006 edges follow Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics). The absence of E003→E004/E005 edges is correct: the frontend does not build against the ClusterRole manifest, and RBAC availability is enforced as an acceptance-criteria gate on E004/E005 (admin CRUD succeeds / viewer rejected) — exactly the configuration-reference treatment the rules require. E001∥E003 and E001∥E004 parallelism is justified (the investigation only gates field-set-dependent work). No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist, so the rule 7/8/10 mandated-parallelism constraints do not apply. Critical path length 4 (E001→E002→E005→E006) matches the summary frontmatter and the M-standard heuristic (3-4). Scanned epic content for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies — none found beyond the AC-gated RBAC configuration reference.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nComponent/team tuples are clean: E001 (Feature Store / FS Engineering), E002 (UXD / UXD Team), E003-E005 (AI Core Dashboard / Dashboard-Frontend Engineering — same team and component, correctly split by sub-system: manifests vs. management views vs. create wizard), E006 (Documentation / Documentation Team). All four component names verified against `.context/rhai-components.txt`. No cross-team bundling; no scope duplication between E004 (list/detail/delete/status) and E005 (create wizard) — the surfaces are disjoint; no work-product type mixing (E002 is pure design, E006 pure content, E003-E005 pure code).\n\nTwo minor observations, neither a boundary violation: (a) E004's scope includes pod-resource monitoring in QE environments — a verification activity the strategy's impacted-teams table assigns to the QE team — bundled as an AC on the implementing team's epic; the summary explicitly justifies QE-as-ACs rather than a separate epic, and the AC is measurable, so this is acceptable. (b) E004 bundles four UI surfaces (list, detail, delete flow, status tracking) and is the largest epic in the set; with the validated prototype (PR #7013) as reference and shared data plumbing, it remains plausibly within a single sprint.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcomes (TP field set, backend restrictions, prototype reuse, CRD stability) change what E002 and E005 do, and both depend on it. E002-E006 are Implementations producing concrete artifacts (design package, manifests, pages, wizard, docs topic). Gating metadata is complete and consistent: E002 and E005 both set `gated_by: E001` with `gate_failure_impact` populated (action: rewrite, re-scope fallback), and each `gated_by` target (E001) appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. Both gates are true gates — the field-set outcome changes the wireframes'/wizard's scope, not merely their schedule. No epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`. The RBAC-mechanism \"needs validation\" assumption folded into E003 scope is acceptable: either resolution delivers the same outcome (admin write path) within the same epic, so it is not a structural gate.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal fields are type-appropriate everywhere: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); E002-E006 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). All six ai-signals rationale files exist, and the rationales match the frontmatter values and the strategy content. Notably consistent: E002's `open_questions: -1` correctly mirrors E005's treatment of the same upstream gate; E005's `change_specificity: 0` / `adapter_pattern: +1` pair is well-reasoned (field set unfixed, prototype as reference implementation); E004's `open_questions: 0` is correct (views render whatever CR fields exist); E003's `open_questions: -1` matches the \"needs validation\" RBAC mechanism assumption. One borderline value noted as a minor: E001's `source_accessibility: 1` is slightly generous — the CRD v1 stability confirmation is a human commitment from Feature Store Engineering rather than a readable source — but the dominant deliverable (form-field spec from the CRD OpenAPI schema and prototype source) is source-derived, and the rationale acknowledges the human element via `human_judgment_required: -1`, so the value remains defensible.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's proposed acceptance criteria (list matching `kubectl get featurestores -A`, status reflecting `.status.conditions` within the watch/poll cycle, delete verified by NotFound, viewer controls absent, air-gapped deployment measurement). Rule-mandated ACs are present wherever applicable: feature-flag/progressive-rollout ACs on E003, E004, and E005 (backwards-compatibility NFR); technical-review-by-engineering AC on E006 (docs-authoring); konflux build-pipeline AC not applicable (no onboarding chain — both components already in the platform). No konflux chain exists, so no AC is missing there. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (verified by scan) — cross-epic references are described by deliverable name (\"form-field specification\", \"finalized UXD wireframes\"), which survives Jira creation correctly.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nEvery strategy capability has an owning epic: create wizard (E005), list/inspect/delete/status views (E004), RBAC extension + feature flag (E003), UXD design (E002), CRD/operator validation (E001), admin-guide documentation (E006). All 7 proposed acceptance criteria from the strategy appear as epic ACs. Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all five risks are mitigated in epic scope (UXD delay → prototype as design reference in E002; CRD schema change → E001 stability confirmation; air-gapped validation completeness → E005 server-side error surfacing; pod resource growth → E004 monitoring AC; viewer RBAC bypass → E003 K8s-level enforcement AC); the \"needs validation\" assumptions are dispositioned (RBAC mechanism → E003 scope, CRD stability → E001, prototype alignment → E001); all 8 open questions have explicit dispositions in the summary, with the two non-technical ones (UXD timeline, performance targets) correctly flagged as health warnings rather than Investigations. Out-of-scope items (edit flow, permissions UI, multi-tenant, Feast UI embedding, new BFF) are respected by the epic set. Documentation Support is listed as \"Pending human review\" in the strategy's prerequisites and a docs-authoring epic (E006) is present regardless, satisfying Rule 11. No conditional branches are used, and none are needed — the summary justifies why both bounded investigation outcomes land in the same epics.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":36831,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7e2f3fa4b1ae564","agent_id":"aac12a4caddf1c229","session_id":"6ff6a9df-bf42-414a-b8db-47534bab81b1","uuid":"2c4bb566-5262-4c75-8a0a-bfad4145a923","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:16:28.742Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8007"}
